{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born in 1140. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli after his death in 1152. Raymond III's early life is not well documented but it is known that he was raised in the court of his father.\n\nRaymond III's reign as Count of Tripoli was marked by conflicts with the neighboring Muslim states. In 1153 he was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Inab. Raymond III was released from captivity in 1153 after a ransom was paid. After his release Raymond III continued to fight against the Muslim states and in 1164 he captured the city of Arzghan.\n\n*Regency of Jerusalem*\nIn 1174 Raymond III became the regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem for King Baldwin IV who was a minor at the time. Raymond III's regency was marked by conflicts with the King's mother Agnes of Courtenay and her second husband Reginald of Sidon. Raymond III also had to deal with the threat of Saladin who was the Sultan of Egypt and Syria.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\nRaymond III died on September 1187 during the Battle of Hattin. He was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. Raymond III's death marked the beginning of the end of the County of Tripoli which was eventually conquered by the Mamluks in 1289.\n\n*Capture of Raymond III*\nRaymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Inab in 1153. He was released from captivity in 1153 after a ransom was paid.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004263237118721008}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}